    E-mail: editor@chronicle.merit.edu.

===============================================================
                     THE CHRONICLE OF HIGHER EDUCATION
         November 24, 1993 (Volume 40, Issue 14) pg. a16, 131 lines.

                                                     Information Technology


                        Free Speech vs. Computer Rules
        Case could offer guide to balancing free speech and computer
                                  resources

                              By David L. Wilson

     A graduate student at the University of Texas at Dallas has filed a
$2-million lawsuit against the university, charging that its officials
violated his First Amendment rights by barring him from the Internet and the
campus computer network.

     Many students have threatened to sue institutions that have denied them
access to computer networks, but this is believed to be the first lawsuit
actually to be filed. If the case goes to trial, the outcome could provide
guidance to colleges that are struggling to balance the right to free speech
with concern over computer security and the use of computer resources.
	
     Officials at UT-Dallas said that the electrical-engineering student,
Gregory N. Steshenko, had been warned several times that his missives to
electronic bulletin boards on Russian and Ukrainian politics were not
permitted under university rules. Those rules restrict the use of campus
computers to activities that are directly related to educational purposes.

     University officials said they did not monitor students' use of
electronic mail and other computer tools, assuming that students were in
compliance unless they received a report that someone was violating the
rules, at which point the student received counseling.

     Robert H. Rutford, the university's president, said the institution had
received complaints about Mr. Steshenko's postings on bulletin boards and
had asked him to stop, but he refused. "The bottom line is, the guy said
he's not going to change his _modus operandi,_" said Mr. Rutford. At a state
university, he added, "there are certain rules and regulations that we have
to comply with, and that's what we're trying to do."

     Mr. Steshenko, who said he had been deported from the Soviet Union in
1986 for being a political dissident, argued that the university had acted
as a censor. Officials have muzzled him, he said, because of complaints from
Ukrainian nationalists who took issue with his perspective on events in that
part of the world.

     "They don't like what I say, and they get this university to stop me,"
Mr. Steshenko said. "These things are not supposed to happen in this
country."

No stranger to controversy

     Mr. Rutford vehemently disagreed, saying that the university had not
pulled the plug on Mr. Steshenko's Internet connection to hush him up. "I
don't care what he says, as long as he's not using university equipment to
say it," Mr. Rutford said.

     Mr. Steshenko filed the lawsuit himself, but he hopes to have a lawyer
if the case actually goes to trial. He has asked for assistance from the
American Civil Liberties Union and groups that are interested in breaking
new ground on the law as it relates to computers.

     Mr. Steshenko is no stranger to such controversies. Earlier this year
he was fired from his job as a software engineer with Microsoft Corporation
in Seattle after complaints about his commentaries reached company officials.

     Mr. Steshenko said he didn't hold a grudge against Microsoft. "Private
companies are not subject to the First Amendment, according to the Supreme
Court, unlike the University of Texas," he said.

     After losing his job, Mr. Steshenko returned to his home in Dallas to
pursue a graduate degree. Since he has been denied access to the campus
computer network, he is worried that he won't be able to complete his
academic work. "I can't talk to my professors," he said. "They all use
electronic mail for assignments and to talk with students."

     Mr. Rutford said that this was not the first time such action had been
taken against a student at UT-Dallas, and that students could get along
perfectly well at the university without electronic mail. "It's not
required," he said.

     Administrators spent a great deal of time making sure that pulling the
plug would not prevent Mr. Steshenko from doing his schoolwork, Mr. Rutford
said. "We talked to the people in the academic programs, we talked to the
deans -- this was not just a knee-jerk decision."

     Mr. Steshenko's detractors have characterized his public writings as
abusive, vulgar, and possibly libelous. He has, for instance, implied that
President Boris Yeltsin of Russia and President Leonid Kravchuk of Ukraine
are lovers. Mr. Steshenko said that he was participating in a vigorous
political discussion, and that his opponents are trying to silence him.

`Different perspective'

     John B. Harlan, an Internet user familiar with Mr. Steshenko's
postings, said it was possible that both characterizations of Mr.
Steshenko's pronouncements contained elements of truth. Mr. Harlan, who is
the campuswide information-system coordinator at Miami University in Ohio,
is the founder of the two electronic mailing lists to which Mr. Steshenko
posted his screeds.

     Mr. Harlan said that he had occasionally acted as a moderator on the
mailing lists, but that in general he had let discussions run their course.
When Mr. Steshenko returned from a trip to Ukraine in the spring and began
posting his negative impressions of post-Soviet life there, Mr. Harlan was
flooded with electronic mail from Ukrainian nationalists demanding that Mr.
Steshenko be silenced.

     "I guess in Eastern Europe there is a somewhat different perspective
about what constitutes freedom of speech," Mr. Harlan said.

     Despite his refusal to block Mr. Steshenko's postings, Mr. Harlan said
he would not have been sorry if Mr. Steshenko had stopped. "In the best of
all possible worlds, Greg would have wandered off and found another forum to
do all this stuff," he said with a laugh. "But both my parents are
librarians, and I really do take freedom of speech very seriously. I'm not
about to tell him that he can't express himself in America, the land of the
free and home of the brave."

     At the same time, Mr. Harlan said he had a great deal of sympathy for
his counterparts in the academic computing center at Texas. "The lines are
really hard to define in these situations. Is this a free-speech issue, or
is it an appropriate-use-of-resources issue?"

     A Washington lawyer familiar with telecommunications law said Mr.
Steshenko's lawsuit could be attractive to legal activists who have been
looking for a free-speech case involving computers that did not include
other factors, such as hacking or harassment. "This could be the one they've
been looking for," said the lawyer, who did not wish to be identified.

A legal minefield

     Carl M. Kadie, co-editor of "Computers and Academic Freedom," an
on-line newsletter, said a court could find that Texas selectively enforced
its educational-use-only policy for its computers.

     "They can't only do this to people who are the target of complaints,
because if they do that they put themselves in the position of censoring
what's unpopular, which is _verboten_ at a state school," said Mr. Kadie, a
graduate student in computer science at the University of Illinois at
Urbana-Champaign.

     The concept of deciding what is educational and what is not at a
university is a legal minefield, he added. "Do they go to people in the
library and say, `Hey, is that book for a class? Or are you just reading it
for fun?'"

